A||||5||FieroMontreal||Problem with Tail lights...||||1||
Z||000000||FieroMontreal||12-02-2001||10:32 PM||||Hey guys..<BR>I'm having a problem with my new 87 SC.. it's running like a new car.. well.. almost  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but I am having a problem with the tail lights. They arn't working. All of the 3 stop lights don't work.. the normal driving lights work and the turn signals work.. but when I hit the brakes.. nothing. <BR>Any ideas?<P>I also have a questiong about the front lights.. I have one 87-88 headlight motor and one 84-86 headligh motor in my car. For some reason one of them does not go as high as the other one. Is this because they are 2 different models of is one of them about to die on me? They both seem to open and close well... I don't know what could cause this.<P>Thanks for your help!<P>------------------<BR> -Alex <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigfieromontreal_1.gif">   <BR>1986 Fiero GT, STOCK Wingless, <BR>President of FieroMontreal... <A HREF="http://www.fieromontreal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieromontreal.com</A> <BR>and Regional Director of CFOG-I for Eastern Canada.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||12-02-2001||10:53 PM||||Park your car in a very quiet place, open your windows and your decklid.  Slowly push on the brake pedal, just enough to trip the switch on it, and listen for a soft relay click.  If you don't, that's probably your problem.  All that the switch under the console does is connect power to fire the relay to turn the lights on.<P>If you don't hear that click, check to make sure that the switch on top of the brake pedal is closing (use meter or test light), and make sure that the connections to the brake light relay are good and tight.  Check the relay too with a meter too.  I belive the relay might be one of the ones that is on the left side of the firewall, but I am not sure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||12-02-2001||11:02 PM||||Not sure if you have a coupe or a fastback, but if it's a coupe and the turn signals are coming on in the back, then you most likely have either a bad or misadjusted switch on the brake pedal.  The switch mounts in a clip that is supposed to automatically adjust it when it's first installed.  If the serrations on the sleeve of the switch are worn out, it may have slipped and not be adjusted anymore.  If you stand on your head under the dash you'll see it mounted near the brake pedal.<BR>The headlight problem is interesting since the 84-86 models used internal torque limiting switches to shut the motor off at the end of the cycle, and the 87-88's used and external electronic module to shut them off when the current draw increases at the end of the cycle.  The wiring and relay setups are very different between the two, so someone must have done a lot of work to make the earlier motor function on yours.  Your best bet might be to replace the early motor with the proper one for your car.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-02-2001||11:07 PM||||sorry, no relay in the brake lights....<P><BR>either the switch is bad or a wire is getting a crappy connection in C500.<P>get a matched set of gen 2 motors on the headlight doors. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroMontreal||12-03-2001||04:49 PM||||Alright, I'll check the switch out ... sometime soon.. damn.. its 4:30 and its dark outside   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> . What should I be looking for exactly? How do I play with it? ... As for the headlights.. I think that 84-86 motor might be leaving me soon... Might be why it's not going up all the way. <P>Edit * yes the car is a coupe<P>New problem today. One of my headlights is burned.. No biggie.. I got to change it and one of the screws is like totaly eaten away... no thread left.. like only a hole.. the screw is rusted and is pretty soft.. any ideas how I can get it out?<BR>Thanks<BR>Alex<P>------------------<BR> -Alex <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigfieromontreal_1.gif">    <BR>1986 Fiero GT, STOCK Wingless, <BR>President of FieroMontreal... <A HREF="http://www.fieromontreal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieromontreal.com</A> <BR>and Regional Director of CFOG-I for Eastern Canada.<p>[This message has been edited by FieroMontreal (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||avengador1||12-03-2001||07:42 PM||||To remove the screws I saw a new screw extractor advertised for Sears by Bob Villa. It is suposed to work on stripped screw heads. You could see if you find this kit at Sears. Hope this helps. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
